    "setup_id": "05601a00c6bc4954635863826740b7b61d18798ad2f99e32a9fef415a652990f",
    "text": "To solve for \\( AC \\) in triangle \\( ABC \\) where \\( \\angle ABC = 90^\\circ \\), \\( AB = 12 \\), and \\( BD = 4 \\), and \\( AD \\) is the angle bisector of \\( \\angle BAC \\), we will use the Angle Bisector Theorem and the Pythagorean Theorem.\n\nFirst, let's denote the lengths of the sides of the triangle:\n- \\( AB = c = 12 \\)\n- \\( BD = 4 \\)\n- \\( DC = BC - BD = x - 4 \\)\n- \\( AC = b \\)\n- \\( BC = a \\)\n\nSince \\( \\angle ABC = 90^\\circ \\), triangle \\( ABC \\) is a right triangle with \\( AB \\) and \\( BC \\) as the legs and \\( AC \\) as the hypotenuse. By the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\\[\na^2 + 12^2 = b^2\n\\]\n\\[\na^2 + 144 = b^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we apply the Angle Bisector Theorem, which states that the angle bisector of an angle in a triangle divides the opposite side into segments that are proportional to the adjacent sides. Therefore:\n\\[\n\\frac{BD}{DC} = \\frac{AB}{AC}\n\\]\nGiven \\( BD = 4 \\) and \\( DC = x - 4 \\), we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{x - 4} = \\frac{12}{b}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( b \\):\n\\[\n4b = 12(x - 4)\n\\]\n\\[\nb = 3(x - 4)\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( b = 3(x - 4) \\) into the Pythagorean Theorem equation:\n\\[\na^2 + 144 = (3(x - 4))^2\n\\]\n\\[\na^2 + 144 = 9(x - 4)^2\n\\]\n\nWe also know that \\( a = BD + DC = 4 + (x - 4) = x \\). Substitute \\( a = x \\) into the equation:\n\\[\nx^2 + 144 = 9(x - 4)^2\n\\]\n\nExpand and simplify:\n\\[\nx^2 + 144 = 9(x^2 - 8x + 16)\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 + 144 = 9x^2 - 72x + 144\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\( x^2 + 144 \\) from both sides:\n\\[\n0 = 8x^2 - 72x\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\( 8x \\):\n\\[\n0 = 8x(x - 9)\n\\]\n\nThus, \\( x = 0 \\) or \\( x = 9 \\). Since \\( x = 0 \\) is not possible (it would imply \\( D \\) is at \\( B \\)), we have:\n\\[\nx = 9\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( x = 9 \\) back to find \\( b \\):\n\\[\nb = 3(x - 4) = 3(9 - 4) = 3 \\times 5 = 15\n\\]\n\nFinally, verify \\( a \\):\n\\[\na = x = 9\n\\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem to find \\( AC \\):\n\\[\na^2 + 12^2 = b^2\n\\]\n\\[\n9^2 + 12^2 = 15^2\n\\]\n\\[\n81 + 144 = 225\n\\]\n\\[\n225 = 225\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of \\( AC \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
